1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,171
Lesson 8.3.

2
00:00:02,551 --> 00:00:05,165
Thermodynamic natural
gradient descent (TNGD).

3
00:00:06,283 --> 00:00:10,978
The natural gradient needs to solve
a linear system at every step, and in

4
00:00:10,978 --> 00:00:15,608
unit six we saw that a stochastic
processing unit, an SPU, solves linear

5
00:00:15,608 --> 00:00:19,531
systems by letting an
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck process equilibrate.

6
00:00:19,911 --> 00:00:23,252
Donatella and colleagues
combined both ideas in

7
00:00:23,252 --> 00:00:27,232
thermodynamic natural gradient
descent, or TNGD: the GPU

8
00:00:27,232 --> 00:00:31,781
computes gradients and curvature,
and the SPU solves the system.

9
00:00:32,883 --> 00:00:37,518
At each iteration, the GPU uses
automatic differentiation to get

10
00:00:37,518 --> 00:00:41,500
the gradient, the output
Jacobian and the loss Hessian.

11
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:45,391
Those factors are loaded into
the SPU through converters.

12
00:00:45,771 --> 00:00:51,743
The SPU evolves according to dg̃
= −((G + λI) g̃ − ∇ℓ) dt plus

13
00:00:51,743 --> 00:00:56,944
noise, whose equilibrium
mean is the natural gradient.

14
00:00:57,324 --> 00:01:00,828
After an analog time t,
the state is read, the GPU

15
00:01:00,828 --> 00:01:03,981
updates the parameters
and the cycle repeats.

16
00:01:04,361 --> 00:01:08,176
The two sides can work in
parallel, asynchronously.

17
00:01:09,283 --> 00:01:13,642
If the SPU starts at the ordinary
gradient, its mean at time t

18
00:01:13,642 --> 00:01:17,931
is a blend: at t = 0 it is the
gradient, the usual stochastic

19
00:01:17,931 --> 00:01:21,305
descent; for large t it
is the natural gradient.

20
00:01:21,685 --> 00:01:26,315
Analog time is a continuous knob
between first and second order.

21
00:01:26,695 --> 00:01:30,774
In a logistic regression with fifty
parameters, the loss after thirty

22
00:01:30,774 --> 00:01:35,266
iterations falls from 0.407 with t =
0, to 0.389 with five relaxation times,

23
00:01:35,646 --> 00:01:48,421
0.378 with twenty, 0.373 with fifty, and
0.368 with the exact natural gradient.

24
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:54,091
A surprise from the paper: not
resetting the SPU between iterations, or

25
00:01:54,091 --> 00:01:58,569
accepting a delay between measurement
and update, improves the result.

26
00:01:58,949 --> 00:02:02,563
The state keeps information about
the previous curvature while

27
00:02:02,563 --> 00:02:05,653
adapting to the new one,
like momentum in optimizers.

28
00:02:06,033 --> 00:02:11,378
In our simulation, with five
relaxation times and a warm

29
00:02:11,378 --> 00:02:15,960
start, the final loss
drops from 0.389 to 0.383.

30
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:19,971
In Donatella's MNIST
experiments, certain delays

31
00:02:19,971 --> 00:02:23,224
even outperform the
exact natural gradient.

32
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:28,235
The cost per iteration is
O(b d_z N + t): building the

33
00:02:28,235 --> 00:02:32,263
Jacobian and Hessian, plus
the analog time, which can be

34
00:02:32,263 --> 00:02:35,860
made very short by designing
small time constants.

35
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:40,030
On MNIST, the authors estimate
about twice Adam's cost

36
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:43,188
per iteration, but with
far fewer iterations.

37
00:02:43,568 --> 00:02:47,719
In DistilBERT fine-tuning,
plain TNGD was worse than Adam,

38
00:02:47,719 --> 00:02:51,370
but the TNGD-Adam variant,
which passes the natural

39
00:02:51,370 --> 00:02:55,020
gradient through Adam's
rule, gave the best result.

40
00:02:56,150 --> 00:02:57,301
Two more advantages.

41
00:02:57,681 --> 00:03:01,587
Conjugate gradient can diverge
on ill-conditioned systems;

42
00:03:01,587 --> 00:03:07,109
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck dynamics converges on
average for any positive definite matrix.

43
00:03:07,489 --> 00:03:10,812
And noise is not an enemy:
it is part of the algorithm.

44
00:03:11,192 --> 00:03:15,924
The proposed hardware uses resistor
arrays for the products with the Jacobian.

45
00:03:16,304 --> 00:03:19,713
The honest limitation: the
results are simulations,

46
00:03:19,713 --> 00:03:22,922
and their impact depends
on large SPUs existing.

47
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:29,959
Use analog time as a hyperparameter between
first and second order, warm-start the SPU

48
00:03:29,959 --> 00:03:34,974
to exploit the momentum effect, and
combine it with Adam in large models.

49
00:03:35,354 --> 00:03:39,844
In lab eleven you will
simulate TNGD on MNIST.

50
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:44,116
Common mistakes: waiting
for full equilibrium at

51
00:03:44,116 --> 00:03:46,886
every step, when short times already help.

52
00:03:47,266 --> 00:03:48,921
Forgetting damping.

53
00:03:49,301 --> 00:03:53,777
Counting only the analog time, when
building the Jacobian also costs.

54
00:03:54,157 --> 00:03:58,305
And presenting simulated speedups
as measured hardware results.

55
00:03:59,417 --> 00:04:03,480
In summary: TNGD splits the work
between a GPU, which computes

56
00:04:03,480 --> 00:04:06,953
gradients and curvature,
and an SPU, which solves the

57
00:04:06,953 --> 00:04:10,689
natural-gradient system with
Ornstein-Uhlenbeck dynamics.

58
00:04:11,069 --> 00:04:14,450
Analog time interpolates
between gradient descent and

59
00:04:14,450 --> 00:04:18,023
natural gradient, delay acts
like momentum, and the cost

60
00:04:18,023 --> 00:04:21,468
per iteration approaches
that of a first-order method.

61
00:04:22,583 --> 00:04:26,219
To practice: write the mean of
the state as a function of time.

62
00:04:26,599 --> 00:04:29,774
Explain why time zero is gradient descent.

63
00:04:30,154 --> 00:04:32,436
Give an explanation of
the momentum effect.

64
00:04:32,816 --> 00:04:35,428
And say which term dominates
the cost per iteration.
